  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\st0098_b1e939993e5b.png\" style=\"width: 151px; height: 115px;\" /><br /><br /><br />Initially,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>E</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mfrac><mi>q</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>g</mi><mi>E</mi></mfrac></math><br />Now, a disc of charge&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&#963;</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>&nbsp;is removed. As <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mo>&#60;</mo><mi>h</mi></math>,&nbsp;we can treat disc as a point charge. The acceleration of the ball is</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>q</mi><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><msub><mi>&#949;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msup><mfenced><mfrac><mi>r</mi><mi>h</mi></mfrac></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p>"
